tributed teachings influenced Neoplatonic writers like Iamblichus, who included her in his biographical accounts of Pythagorean philosophers around 300 CE."}, {"sourceSlug": "pythagoreanism", "sourceName": "Pythagoreanism", "verb": "CONTAINS", "targetSlug": "myia", "targetName": "Myia", "context": "Myia was a prominent member and philosopher within the early Pythagorean school, embodying its ideals of intellectual and ethical living."}], "places": [{"name": "Croton, Magna Graecia", "role": "Center of activity"}, {"name": "Metapontum, Magna Graecia", "role": "Possible residence"}, {"name": "Samos, Ancient Greece", "role": "Birthplace of Pythagoras"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Myia", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "myia"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q456560", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Myia"], "tags": ["Philosophers & Thinkers", "doctrine-development", "cause-and-effect", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Myia holds significant regional and thematic importance as a symbol of early female intellectualism within the Pythagorean school, influencing later narratives of women's contributions to philosophy for centuries. Her attributed teachings reinforced core ethical principles within a major ancient philosophical tradition.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-20T07:37:39Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#27864284286", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Myia was a Pythagorean philosopher and, according to later tradition, one of the daughters of Theano and Pythagoras.", "newValue": "Myia, a figure from the late 6th to early 5th century BCE, is primarily known as a Pythagorean philosopher and a prominent example of female intellectual engagement within the early Pythagorean community. While her historical existence is debated by modern scholars, ancient traditions, particularly from the Neoplatonic era, consistently identify her as one of the daughters of Pythagoras and his wife, Theano. Her significance lies in her representation of women's roles in ancient Greek philosophical circles, especially within the innovative intellectual and spiritual environment of Magna Graecia.

While direct historical accounts from her lifetime are scarce, later biographers and doxographers, notably Iamblichus in his *On the Pythagorean Life* (c. 300 CE), include Myia in their narratives of the Pythagorean school. These sources attribute to her teachings on the importance of moderation and balance (κόσμος) in human life, especially regarding the harmony of the soul and the body, and the proper upbringing of children. Her philosophical contributions, though not preserved in extant texts, were understood to embody the practical ethics and communal ideals central to Pythagoreanism, emphasizing a harmonious and disciplined way of life.

Myia's enduring legacy is less about specific philosophical treatises and more about her symbolic role in the history of philosophy, particularly for women. Her inclusion in lists of prominent female Pythagoreans, alongside figures like Damo, Arignote, and Aesara, contributed to a tradition that highlighted the intellectual capabilities and contributions of women in an era often perceived as exclusively male-dominated. This tradition influenced later philosophical schools and provided a historical precedent for women's participation in intellectual life, inspiring subsequent feminist interpretations of ancient thought."}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-20T07:37:39Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#27864284286", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "600 BCE, Crotone"}, {"title": "Ongoing philosophical inquiry and intellectual debate", "type": "Idea", "year": "600 BCE, Crotone"}], "newValue": ["The establishment of the Pythagorean school in Croton by Pythagoras provided a unique environment for intellectual and spiritual development, including for women.", "Pythagoras's revolutionary teachings on mathematics, philosophy, and communal living attracted a diverse following, including female adherents like Theano.", "The philosophical climate of Magna Graecia in the 6th century BCE fostered an environment where novel intellectual movements could flourish and integrate women into their core structures."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-20T07:37:39Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#27864284286", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Contributed philosophical ideas to ongoing intellectual discourse", "type": "Idea", "year": "500 BCE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "500 BCE"}], "newValue": ["Myia's inclusio